::ALG2_U03_L08_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L08_GP01
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify sqrt(72).
{
=A. 6sqrt(2)#Correct. 72 = 36*2 and the square root of 36 is 6.
~B. 36sqrt(2)#The square root of 36 is 6, not 36.
~C. 8sqrt(9)#The perfect square factor should be rooted.
~D. 12sqrt(6)#This does not square back to 72.
}

::ALG2_U03_L08_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L08_GP02
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the domain of f(x)=sqrt(x-6)?
{
~A. x <\= 6#That allows negative radicands.
=B. x >\= 6#Correct. The radicand must be nonnegative: x - 6 >= 0.
~C. x !\= 6#x = 6 is allowed.
~D. all real numbers#Inputs less than 6 are not allowed.
}

::ALG2_U03_L08_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L08_GP03
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Solve sqrt(x+3)=5.
{
~A. x \= 2#This subtracts from 5 instead of 25.
~B. x \= 28#This adds 3 instead of subtracting it.
=C. x \= 22#Correct. Square both sides to get x + 3 = 25.
~D. x \= 5#This is the square root value, not x.
}

::ALG2_U03_L08_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L08_GP04
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Simplify (x²-16)/(x-4).
{
~A. x - 4, x !\= 4#The remaining factor is x + 4.
~B. x + 4, no restriction#The original denominator excludes 4.
~C. x²+4#The numerator must be factored.
=D. x + 4, x !\= 4#Correct. Factor and cancel x - 4 while keeping the original restriction.
}

::ALG2_U03_L08_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L08_GP05
MLA Standard: Unit 3 standards synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is the vertical asymptote of f(x)=7/(x+2)?
{
=A. x \= -2#Correct. Set x + 2 equal to 0.
~B. x \= 2#The sign is reversed.
~C. y \= -2#Vertical asymptotes use x = value.
~D. y \= 7#The numerator is not the asymptote.
}

